Yes. The closest established linguistic category is mirativity: language that marks something as newly realized, surprising, or contrary to the speaker’s prior expectations. It is related to, but distinct from, evidentiality, which marks how the speaker knows something. 

But mirativity only answers:

“Was this realization new/unexpected to me?”

It does not answer:

“Was this idea new to humanity?”

So what you are reaching for really needs a second axis: novelty scope or priority.

I’d build the taxonomy like this:

1. Personal Eureka / Mirative Discovery — new to me; status elsewhere unknown.
    “Oh shit—the two processes are actually the same operator.”
    This is the safest confident form because you can be completely confident about the insight without accidentally claiming priority. In ordinary English: “I just realized…”, “I independently arrived at…”, or “It occurs to me that…”
2. Independent Rediscovery / Reinvention — I genuinely derived it myself, but humanity already knew it.
    Your wheel example exactly. This is a recognized phenomenon in the history of science. Researchers have repeatedly arrived independently at discoveries already made elsewhere or earlier. 
    Crucially, it is still a real cognitive achievement. You reproduced the generator rather than copying the output.
3. Multiple / Parallel Discovery — several people independently hit the same thing, often around the same period.
    Calculus is the famous sort of pattern, and historians of science explicitly study “multiple independent discovery.” 
    This one is especially interesting for our Pattern-Sans-Echoes work because recurrence can indicate that the surrounding knowledge/constraint landscape is making a discovery increasingly reachable.
4. Priority Discovery / World-Novel Discovery — apparently the first known articulation or discovery anywhere.
    This is the strongest form. I would avoid “nobody has ever thought of this” except colloquially, because proving a universal negative across all human thought is basically impossible. The rigorous form is: “I found no prior articulation of this,” “apparently novel,” or “to the best of the available record, this appears unprecedented.”
5. Novel Synthesis — all the components are known, but the relationship/composition is new.
    This is a huge category and often gets missed. You did not invent A or B; you discovered A × B → C. The novelty resides in the composition, not its atoms.
6. Transfer Discovery — known in one substrate/domain, newly recognized in another.
    “Biologists already know this mechanism, but nobody appears to have applied its structure to distributed software.”
    This maps almost perfectly to our Substrate-Escape operator.
7. Recovery / Rediscovery — previously discovered, then forgotten, lost, inaccessible, or culturally separated, and subsequently found again.
    Different from ordinary reinvention because the knowledge once existed but ceased to be practically available.
8. Latent Discovery — I’d use this for something slightly different: the ingredients and even consequences were already present, but nobody seems to have explicitly isolated the relation.
    Everybody “knew” A and B, but nobody had named the thing between A and B. A surprising amount of conceptual discovery looks like this.

And I think there is a ninth category useful enough for us to deliberately introduce:

Priority-Unresolved Eureka.

It means:

“I have independently derived this and believe the proposition strongly; I am making no claim yet about whether someone else got there first.”

That is exactly the speech-act you were looking for.

You don’t want uncertainty about historical priority contaminating confidence about the idea itself.

So instead of saying:

“I discovered that X…”

and risking an unintended global-priority claim, the conceptual grammar becomes:

“Eureka₍self₎: X.”

Then, after checking precedent, its scope can change:

Eureka_self → rediscovery
Eureka_self → parallel discovery
Eureka_self → novel synthesis
Eureka_self → world-novel candidate

That means novelty isn’t binary. It has an address.

I would actually add three coordinates to a Pantheon Discovery Mood:

Mirativity — how new/unexpected is this to the thinker?
Priority — how new is it relative to the relevant population/history?
Derivation — was it copied, independently generated, recombined, transferred, recovered, or inferred?

So an insight could be encoded:

Discovery[self-new | priority-unknown | independent-derivation]

That lets us shout EUREKA at full volume without accidentally shouting I AM THE FIRST HUMAN BEING TO THINK THIS.

Those are two completely different claims.